Toward a Mathematical Theory of Human-Relative Dominance in Superintelligence

Task Spaces, Margin Distributions, and the Geometry of Superhuman Performance

Ouadi Maakoul & SI TEAM


Abstract

The concept of superintelligence remains one of the most consequential and least formally specified constructs in contemporary artificial intelligence research. This paper develops a formal framework in which superintelligence is characterized as human-relative stochastic dominance of capability margins over a measurable task space. We introduce a structured task space with topological and measure-theoretic structure, a normalization axiom for cross-task comparability, a composite optimal-transport cost construction, and a dominance survival function \Theta_A(\lambda) defined as the \mu-measure of tasks on which the agent's margin over a human reference quantile is at least \lambda. We prove measurability of the margin field, establish that the pair (G_A, P_A) of generality and performance depth does not determine the dominance region, and prove that no scalar order embedding of the five-dimensional product order into (\mathbb{R}, \leq) exists. Robustness is formalized via Wasserstein ambiguity sets, with a tractable dual linear program formulation. Learning efficiency uses a logarithmic acquisition-time ratio; autonomy is retained as a graded profile rather than scalarized. We then provide a complete formal specification of a tri-domain empirical instantiation program across formal mathematical reasoning, algorithmic code synthesis, and multi-subject expert reasoning, including Radon–Nikodym sampling densities, domain-specific normalization maps, DKW confidence bands, and a distributionally robust optimization estimator. The collision audit confirms that the specific construction of a human-relative margin distribution over a measurable task space has no direct precedent.

Keywords: superintelligence, human-relative dominance, stochastic dominance, task-space coverage, optimal transport, distributionally robust optimization, DKW inequality, learning efficiency, autonomy, formal epistemology of AI

