1. Introduction

1.1. The Central Question

The term superintelligence has become one of the most consequential and least formally specified constructs in contemporary discussions of artificial intelligence. Bostrom (2003, 2014) defines it as "any intellect that greatly exceeds the cognitive performance of humans in virtually all domains of interest." The mathematical difficulty is that greatly exceeds and virtually all domains implicitly require a task space, a performance function, a human reference distribution, a measure over the task space, and a rule for determining how much of the task space must satisfy the superiority condition.

This dissertation addresses a narrower question:

\boxed{
\textit{Can superintelligence be defined as a measurable human-relative dominance relation over a structured task space, extended by learning efficiency, robustness, and autonomy?}
}

The objective is to construct a mathematical framework in which claims of superhuman performance can be stated, compared, and falsified.

1.2. The Fundamental Object

The central mathematical object is the dominance survival function:

\boxed{
\Theta_A(\lambda) = \mu\left(\left\{T \in \mathcal{T} : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda\right\}\right),
}

where \mathcal{T} is a structured task space, \mu is a probability measure on \mathcal{T}, M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) is the superhuman margin of agent A over human reference quantile q, \lambda is the intensity, and \Theta_A(\lambda) is the extent. All scalar summaries — including generality, performance depth, and any weighted combination — are derived functionals of \Theta_A.

1.3. Methodological Position

This paper adopts a strict prior-art principle:

\boxed{
\text{No novelty claim without systematic collision audit.}
}

The collision audit (Section 6) confirms that while the mathematical pattern of survival/coverage functions over task collections is established in performance profiles (Dolan & Moré, 2002) and empirical attainment functions (Fonseca et al., 2011), the specific construction

\Theta_A(\lambda) = \mu\left[\, p_A(T) - p_H^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda \,\right]

— where the reference is human-relative, the threshold is a performance margin, and \mu is a measure over a structured cognitive task space — has no direct precedent.

