2. Historical and Mathematical Foundations

2.1. Universal Intelligence

Legg and Hutter (2007) proposed:

\Upsilon(\pi) = \sum_{\mu} 2^{-K(\mu)} V^{\pi}_{\mu}.

The measure is architecture-independent but uncomputable, and it compresses heterogeneous capability into a scalar. Two agents with identical \Upsilon may possess radically different profiles of specialization, generalization, and adaptation.

2.2. Capability–Generality Separation

Hernández-Orallo et al. (2021) formalized the distinction between capability (area under the performance curve over task difficulty) and generality (inverse dispersion of performance across difficulty levels) and demonstrated their empirical decoupling. Our G_A and P_A functionals differ in that they are derived from a human-relative dominance survival function rather than from the internal shape of the agent's performance curve.

2.3. Multidimensional Intelligence Spaces

Ko (2019) proposed representing intelligent systems as regions in a multidimensional cognitive space, with superintelligence characterized geometrically by inclusion of the human region as a proper subset. Morris et al. (2024) organized AGI capabilities around depth of performance, breadth of generality, and autonomy. We do not claim multidimensionality as novel.

2.4. Performance Profiles and Attainment Functions

Dolan and Moré (2002) introduced performance profiles:

\rho_s(\tau) = \frac{1}{n_p}\left|\left\{p : \frac{t_{p,s}}{\min_{s'} t_{p,s'}} \leq \tau\right\}\right|.

The empirical attainment function (Fonseca et al., 2011) is

\alpha_T(z) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \mathbf{1}_{\{X_i \leq_T z\}}.

Both are coverage functions over task collections, but the reference is the best solver or the algorithm ensemble, not a human population, and the threshold is a relative ratio or an objective vector, not a human-relative margin.

2.5. Task-Level Stochastic Dominance

Stochastic dominance has been used to compare AI and human accuracy distributions on single tasks. The construction X_A = M_{A,H}(T) \sim \mu over a structured task space has no direct precedent.

2.6. Superhuman Adaptable Intelligence

Goldfeder et al. (2026) introduce SAI, defining it as systems learning to exceed humans on important capabilities. Their framework places adaptation speed at the center of an alternative operational conception of advanced AI. SAI and the present framework are complementary: SAI asks how rapidly can an agent become superhuman on new tasks?; the present framework asks on what measurable fraction of task space, by what margin, under what reference and robustness conditions, is the agent dominant?

