3. The Structured Task Space

3.1. Definition of a Task

Definition 3.1 (Task). A task is a tuple

T = (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{C}, \tau, \nu),

where \mathcal{X} is the observation space, \mathcal{Y} the action space, \mathcal{E} the environment, \mathcal{R} the utility functional, \mathcal{C} the resource constraint, \tau the horizon, and \nu the novelty condition.

3.2. The Task Space

Definition 3.2 (Structured Task Space). The task space is a tuple

\mathfrak{T} = (\mathcal{T}, \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}, \mu),

where \mathcal{T} is a set of admissible tasks, \tau_{\mathcal{T}} is a topology on \mathcal{T}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} is a \sigma-algebra containing \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, and \mu: \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} \to [0,1] is a probability measure with \mu(\mathcal{T}) = 1.

Axiom T1 (Non-degeneracy). For every non-empty open set U \in \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, \mu(U) > 0.

Axiom T2 (Representation invariance). Equivalent representations of the same cognitive task induce the same measure.

Axiom T3 (Difficulty sensitivity). The measure admits explicit representation of task difficulty.

Axiom T4 (Domain coverage). The measure assigns nonzero mass to each major cognitive domain.

Axiom T5 (Stability). Small admissible changes in the task-generation protocol do not cause arbitrarily large changes in the dominance classification.

3.3. Composite Task Cost

Definition 3.3 (Task Cost). We commit to the following composite cost construction:

\boxed{
c(T, T') = \alpha \cdot d_{\mathcal{X}}(T, T') + \beta \cdot d_{\mathcal{Y}}(T, T') + \gamma \cdot d_{\mathcal{R}}(T, T'),
}

where d_{\mathcal{X}}, d_{\mathcal{Y}}, d_{\mathcal{R}} are Wasserstein distances on observation distributions, action distributions under a fixed reference policy class, and optimal-policy differences under a fixed reference agent class \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ref}}, and \alpha, \beta, \gamma > 0.

Assumption 3.3a (Regularity). The cost c is symmetric, lower semi-continuous with respect to \tau_{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, vanishes on the diagonal, and satisfies c(T, T') > 0 for T \neq T'.

Under Assumption 3.3a, the c-Wasserstein distance W_c is well-defined as a metric on \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}).

3.4. Human Reference

Definition 3.4 (Human Reference). For each task T \in \mathcal{T}, let P_T be a task-conditioned human population with performance distribution F_{P_T}. The human reference quantile of order q \in (0,1) is

p_H^{(q)}(T) = F_{P_T}^{-1}(q).

3.5. Normalization Axiom

Axiom N (Normalization compatibility). A normalization is a family \{\phi_T\}_{T \in \mathcal{T}}, \phi_T: \mathbb{R} \to [0,1], such that:

(N1) \phi_T is monotone;

(N2) \phi_T preserves quantile ordering: \phi_T(s_H^{(q)}) = q;

(N3) \phi_T is continuous.

Under Axiom N, the normalized human reference satisfies p_H^{(q)}(T) = q, and the margin

M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) = \phi_T(s_A(T)) - q

is comparable across tasks.

