4. The Dominance Survival Function

4.1. Measurability

Assumption 4.0a (Joint measurability). The map (T, s) \mapsto \phi_T(s) is jointly measurable with respect to \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}).

Lemma 4.0 (Measurability of the margin field). If T \mapsto s_A(T) and T \mapsto s_H^{(q)}(T) are \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable, then T \mapsto M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

Proof. The composition T \mapsto \phi_T(s_A(T)) is measurable by Lemma A.1 (composition of measurable maps). Subtraction of the constant q preserves measurability. \square

4.2. The Dominance Survival Function

Definition 4.1 (Margin Field). Define X_A = M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T), T \sim \mu.

Definition 4.2 (Dominance Survival Function). For \lambda \in \mathbb{R}:

\boxed{
\Theta_A(\lambda) = \mu\left(\left\{T \in \mathcal{T} : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda\right\}\right) = \Pr(X_A \geq \lambda).
}

The parameter \lambda represents intensity; the value \Theta_A(\lambda) represents extent.

4.3. Dominance Region and Quantile

Definition 4.3 (Dominance Region). \mathcal{D}_A = \{(\lambda, \theta) : \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta\}.

Definition 4.4 (Dominance Quantile). For \theta \in (0,1], Q_A(\theta) = \sup\{\lambda : \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta\}.

Proposition 4.1 (Duality). Q_A(\theta) \geq \lambda \iff \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta.

4.4. Generality and Performance Depth

Definition 4.5 (Generality). G_A = \Theta_A(0).

Definition 4.6 (Performance Depth). P_A = \operatorname{ess\,sup}(X_A).

Remark 4.1 (Non-attainment). The supremum P_A need not be attained. In general \Theta_A(P_A) = 0, and the pair (G_A, P_A) does not correspond to a point in \mathcal{D}_A.

Proposition 4.2 (Non-determination). (G_A, P_A) does not determine \mathcal{D}_A.

Proof. Construct two agents with equal (G, P) but different \Theta. Let \mu be Lebesgue on [0,1], M_A(T) = 0.5 \cdot \mathbf{1}_{[0.5,1]}(T) - 0.5 \cdot \mathbf{1}_{[0,0.5)}(T), and M_B(T) = 0.5 \cdot \mathbf{1}_{[0.5,1]}(T) - 1.0 \cdot \mathbf{1}_{[0,0.5)}(T). Then G_A = G_B = 0.5 and P_A = P_B = 0.5, but \Theta_A \neq \Theta_B. \square

Corollary 4.1. \Theta_A is strictly more informative than (G_A, P_A).

