8. Learning Efficiency and Autonomy

8.1. Learning Efficiency

Definition 8.1 (Acquisition Time). \tau_A(T; \eta) = \inf\{n : p(A, T \mid n) \geq \eta\}.

Definition 8.2 (Logarithmic Ratio).

\boxed{
E_{A/H}(T; \eta) = \log\left(\frac{\tau_H(T; \eta) + \delta}{\tau_A(T; \eta) + \delta}\right),
}

with \delta > 0. The logarithmic form is bounded, symmetric under exchange, and well-defined for infinite acquisition times.

Definition 8.3 (Global Learning Efficiency). E_A(\eta) = \int_{\mathcal{T}} E_{A/H}(T; \eta) \, d\mu(T).

8.2. Autonomy — Retained as Profile

Definition 8.4 (Autonomy Axis). \mathcal{A}: \mathcal{T} \to \{0,1,2,3\}, with 0 = full specification, 1 = goal specified / method unspecified, 2 = goal under-specified / decomposition required, 3 = only environment given / goal generation required.

Definition 8.5 (Autonomy Profile). \mathbf{A}_A = (A_0, A_1, A_2, A_3), where A_k = \mu(\{T : \mathcal{A}(T) \geq k\}).

Remark 8.1 (No scalarization). We deliberately do not define A_A = \sum_k w_k A_k. Any such scalarization reintroduces an arbitrary weight vector and contradicts the spirit of Theorem 5.1.

Definition 8.6 (Autonomy-Constrained Dominance).

\Theta_A^{\mathrm{auto}}(\lambda, k) = \mu\left(\left\{T : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda \text{ and } \mathcal{A}(T) \geq k\right\}\right).

Proposition 8.1. \Theta_A^{\mathrm{auto}}(\lambda, k) is non-increasing in k, and \Theta_A^{\mathrm{auto}}(\lambda, 0) = \Theta_A(\lambda).

