10. Falsifiability and Open Problems

10.1. Falsifiability Levels

Level 1 (Empirical). A claim that \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta is refuted if the DKW lower band satisfies \widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}^{\mathrm{lower}}(\lambda) < \theta.

Level 2 (Structural). The multidimensional decomposition is refuted if the five dimensions prove empirically inseparable.

Level 3 (Representation). Classification is refuted if it depends arbitrarily on task representation under transformations preserving cognitive content.

Level 4 (Measure sensitivity). Classification is refuted if admissible perturbation of \mu reverses it, unless \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}} is adopted.

10.2. Open Problems

Problem 10.1 (Canonical cost). Characterize weights (\alpha, \beta, \gamma, w_d) for which \mathcal{D}_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\varepsilon) is invariant up to O(\varepsilon).

Problem 10.2 (Canonical measure). Characterize the family \mathfrak{M} of admissible \mu satisfying Axioms T1–T5. Determine whether a canonical element (maximum entropy, complexity-weighted) exists.

Problem 10.3 (Sharpness of robust dominance). Determine the maximal \varepsilon^* such that \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon^*) > 0.

Problem 10.4 (Invariance). Characterize the group G of transformations \phi: \mathfrak{T} \to \mathfrak{T}' preserving the dominance classification.

Problem 10.5 (Reconstruction rate). Determine whether the DKW rate n^{-1/2} is minimax-optimal for estimating \Theta_A in the sup-norm on infinite task spaces.

Problem 10.6 (Minimality). Is the five-dimensional feasible region \mathcal{F}_A minimal?

Problem 10.7 (Order completion). Does \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} admit a canonical linear extension?

Problem 10.8 (Legg–Hutter bridge). Under what conditions on \mu does \Theta_A^K dominate \Upsilon?

Problem 10.9 (Chollet relation). Formalize the relationship between E_A(\eta) and Chollet's skill-acquisition efficiency.

Problem 10.10 (Empirical instantiation). Complete the tri-domain program of Section 9: define \mu explicitly for each domain, compute \widehat{\Theta}_A for frontier models at q = 0.9, and test for stochastic dominance between models.

---

11. Conclusion

The principal results of this paper are:

First, the fundamental object is the dominance survival function \Theta_A(\lambda) = \mu(\{M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda\}).

Second, the pair (G_A, P_A) are derived functionals of \Theta_A, and (G_A, P_A) does not determine \Theta_A (Proposition 4.2).

Third, no scalar order embedding of the product order into (\mathbb{R}, \leq) preserves all order relations (Theorem 5.1). The stochastic dominance relation is a preorder, becoming a partial order only on equivalence classes.

Fourth, robustness is formalized via Wasserstein ambiguity sets, with a tractable dual linear program formulation (Definition 7.1, Theorem 7.1, Section 9.8).

Fifth, learning efficiency uses a logarithmic acquisition-time ratio; autonomy is retained as a graded profile, deliberately not scalarized.

Sixth, the dominance survival function is estimable from finite samples with DKW-uniform error bounds \varepsilon_n(\alpha) = \sqrt{\ln(2/\alpha)/(2n)}, and the DRO robust version admits a dual LP characterization.

Seventh, a complete tri-domain empirical instantiation program across formal mathematics, code synthesis, and expert reasoning has been specified, including Radon–Nikodym sampling densities, domain-specific normalization maps, and an explicit execution protocol.

Eighth, the collision audit confirms that the specific construction of a human-relative margin distribution over a measurable task space has no direct precedent. The mathematical pattern of survival/coverage functions is established, but its instantiation as human-relative stochastic dominance of capability margins over a structured cognitive task space is novel.

The framework does not claim to resolve the problem of superintelligence. It converts the problem into a form in which partial results can be stated rigorously, open problems can be formulated precisely, empirical claims can be tested, and refutation conditions can be specified.

\boxed{
\textbf{Novel claim: human-relative stochastic dominance of capability margins over a measurable task space.}
