Appendix A — Standing Assumptions and Measurability

A.1. Standing Assumptions

Throughout, let \mathfrak{T} = (\mathcal{T}, \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}, \mu) be a structured task space as in Definition 3.2, with \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} a \sigma-algebra containing the Borel \sigma-algebra generated by \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, and \mu a probability measure on (\mathcal{T}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}).

We assume:

(MA1) For each agent A, the raw-score field T \mapsto s_A(T) \in \mathbb{R} is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

(MA2) For each quantile order q \in (0,1), the human reference field T \mapsto s_H^{(q)}(T) \in \mathbb{R} is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

(MA3) The normalization family \{\phi_T\}_{T \in \mathcal{T}}, with \phi_T: \mathbb{R} \to [0,1], is jointly measurable with respect to \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}): the map (T, s) \mapsto \phi_T(s) is measurable.

(MA4) For every T \in \mathcal{T}, \phi_T is continuous and monotone (non-decreasing for higher-is-better tasks; non-increasing for lower-is-better tasks).

---

A.2. Lemma A.1 (Composition of measurable maps)

Lemma A.1. Let f: \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R} be \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable, and let \psi: \mathcal{T} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} be jointly measurable. Then T \mapsto \psi(T, f(T)) is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

Proof. Define \Phi: \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{T} \times \mathbb{R} by \Phi(T) = (T, f(T)). For any E \in \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}), we may write E as a countable union of rectangles A_i \times B_i with A_i \in \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} and B_i \in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}). Then

\Phi^{-1}(A_i \times B_i) = A_i \cap f^{-1}(B_i) \in \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}},

because A_i \in \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} and f^{-1}(B_i) \in \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} by measurability of f. Countable unions preserve \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurability, so \Phi is measurable. The composition \psi \circ \Phi is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable as a composition of measurable maps. \square

---

A.3. Lemma A.2 (Measurability of the margin field)

Lemma A.2. Under (MA1)–(MA4), the map

T \mapsto M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) = \phi_T(s_A(T)) - q

is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

Proof. Apply Lemma A.1 with f = s_A (measurable by MA1) and \psi(T, s) = \phi_T(s) (jointly measurable by MA3). The composition T \mapsto \phi_T(s_A(T)) is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable. Subtraction of the constant q \in \mathbb{R} preserves measurability. \square

---

A.4. Corollary A.3 (Measurability of superhuman sets)

Corollary A.3. For every \lambda \in \mathbb{R}, the superhuman set

S_\lambda(A; H_q) = \{T \in \mathcal{T} : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda\}

is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

Proof. The interval [\lambda, \infty) is Borel. The preimage under a \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable map is in \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}. \square

---

A.5. Lemma A.4 (Monotonicity and right-continuity of \Theta_A)

Lemma A.4. The map \lambda \mapsto \Theta_A(\lambda) is non-increasing, right-continuous, and satisfies \lim_{\lambda \to -\infty} \Theta_A(\lambda) = 1 and \lim_{\lambda \to +\infty} \Theta_A(\lambda) = 0 almost surely.

Proof. Monotonicity. For \lambda_1 \leq \lambda_2, \{M \geq \lambda_2\} \subseteq \{M \geq \lambda_1\}, hence \Theta_A(\lambda_2) \leq \Theta_A(\lambda_1).

Right-continuity. For any decreasing sequence \lambda_n \downarrow \lambda, the sets \{M \geq \lambda_n\} are decreasing with intersection \{M \geq \lambda\}. Continuity of \mu from above yields \Theta_A(\lambda_n) \to \Theta_A(\lambda).

Limits. As \lambda \to -\infty, \{M \geq \lambda\} \uparrow \mathcal{T}, so \Theta_A(\lambda) \to \mu(\mathcal{T}) = 1. As \lambda \to +\infty, \{M \geq \lambda\} \downarrow \varnothing, so \Theta_A(\lambda) \to 0, provided X_A is finite \mu-almost surely. \square

