Appendix B — Proofs for Section 4

B.1. Proof of Proposition 4.1 (Duality of \Theta_A and Q_A)

Proposition 4.1. For all \theta \in (0,1] and \lambda \in \mathbb{R}:

Q_A(\theta) \geq \lambda \iff \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta.

Proof.

Direction (\Rightarrow). Suppose Q_A(\theta) \geq \lambda. By Definition 4.4, Q_A(\theta) = \sup\{\lambda' : \Theta_A(\lambda') \geq \theta\}, so there exists a sequence \lambda_n' \uparrow Q_A(\theta) with \Theta_A(\lambda_n') \geq \theta. Since Q_A(\theta) \geq \lambda and \Theta_A is non-increasing (Lemma A.4), for any \lambda \leq Q_A(\theta) we have \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \Theta_A(Q_A(\theta)^-). Taking the limit as n \to \infty:

\Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \lim_{n \to \infty} \Theta_A(\lambda_n') \geq \theta.

Direction (\Leftarrow). Suppose \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta. Then \lambda \in \{\lambda' : \Theta_A(\lambda') \geq \theta\}, so Q_A(\theta) = \sup\{\cdots\} \geq \lambda. \square

---

B.2. Proof of Proposition 4.2 (Non-determination of \mathcal{D}_A by (G_A, P_A))

Proposition 4.2. The pair (G_A, P_A) does not determine \mathcal{D}_A.

Proof. We construct two agents A, B with the same (G, P) but different dominance survival functions.

Let \mathcal{T} = [0,1] with \mu Lebesgue measure. Define margin fields:

M_A(T) = \begin{cases} -0.5 & T \in [0, 0.5), \\ 0.5 & T \in [0.5, 1]. \end{cases}

M_B(T) = \begin{cases} -1.0 & T \in [0, 0.5), \\ 0.5 & T \in [0.5, 1]. \end{cases}

Compute survival functions:

\Theta_A(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq -0.5, \\ 0.5 & -0.5 < \lambda \leq 0.5, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.5. \end{cases}

\Theta_B(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq -1.0, \\ 0.5 & -1.0 < \lambda \leq 0.5, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.5. \end{cases}

Compare:

· G_A = \Theta_A(0) = 0.5 = \Theta_B(0) = G_B.
· P_A = \operatorname{ess\,sup} X_A = 0.5 = \operatorname{ess\,sup} X_B = P_B.

But: For \lambda \in (-0.5, 0], \Theta_A(\lambda) = 0.5, while \Theta_B(\lambda) = 0.5 as well since -0.5 > -1.0. Wait, this fails to distinguish.

We adjust: set M_B(T) = -0.5 \cdot \mathbf{1}_{[0, 0.75)}(T) + 0.5 \cdot \mathbf{1}_{[0.75, 1]}(T). Then:

\Theta_B(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq -0.5, \\ 0.25 & -0.5 < \lambda \leq 0.5, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.5. \end{cases}

Now G_A = 0.5, G_B = 0.25. Still unequal.

Corrected construction. For equal G, we need \Theta_A(0) = \Theta_B(0). Set:

M_A(T) = \begin{cases} -0.5 & T \in [0, 0.5), \\ 0.5 & T \in [0.5, 1]. \end{cases}

M_B(T) = \begin{cases} -1.0 & T \in [0, 0.25) \cup [0.5, 0.75), \\ 0.5 & T \in [0.25, 0.5) \cup [0.75, 1]. \end{cases}

Then:

\Theta_A(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq -0.5, \\ 0.5 & -0.5 < \lambda \leq 0.5, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.5. \end{cases}

\Theta_B(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq -1.0, \\ 0.5 & -1.0 < \lambda \leq 0.5, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.5. \end{cases}

Check: G_A = G_B = 0.5. P_A = P_B = 0.5. But \Theta_A(\lambda) \neq \Theta_B(\lambda) for \lambda \in (-1.0, -0.5], where \Theta_B(\lambda) = 0.5 but \Theta_A(\lambda) = 1. \square

---

B.3. Corollary B.3.1 (Non-attainment of P_A)

Corollary B.3.1. The supremum P_A = \operatorname{ess\,sup}(X_A) need not be attained, and \Theta_A(P_A) = 0 in general.

Proof. Consider M_A(T) = 1 - T for T \in (0,1] with \mu = \mathrm{Leb}. Then:

\Theta_A(\lambda) = \mu(\{T : 1 - T \geq \lambda\}) = \mu(\{T : T \leq 1 - \lambda\}) = 1 - \lambda

for \lambda \in [0,1], and \Theta_A(\lambda) = 0 for \lambda > 1. Then P_A = 1 but \Theta_A(1) = 0. \square

---

B.4. Proposition B.4.1 (AUC equals mean margin)

Proposition B.4.1. If X_A \in [-q, 1-q] almost surely, then

\int_{-q}^{1-q} \Theta_A(\lambda) \, d\lambda = \mathbb{E}[X_A] + q.

Proof. By Fubini's theorem and the definition \Theta_A(\lambda) = \Pr(X_A \geq \lambda):

\int_{-q}^{1-q} \Theta_A(\lambda) \, d\lambda = \int_{-q}^{1-q} \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_{\{X_A \geq \lambda\}}] \, d\lambda = \mathbb{E}\left[\int_{-q}^{1-q} \mathbf{1}_{\{X_A \geq \lambda\}} \, d\lambda\right].

The inner integral equals the Lebesgue measure of [-q, X_A], which is X_A + q when X_A \in [-q, 1-q]. Therefore:

\int_{-q}^{1-q} \Theta_A(\lambda) \, d\lambda = \mathbb{E}[X_A + q] = \mathbb{E}[X_A] + q.

\square

Remark B.4.1. The AUC is the mean margin shifted by q. It is a scalar summary of \Theta_A and thus loses the structural information of the full survival function.

