Appendix C — Proofs for Section 5

C.1. Proof of Theorem 5.1 (Order-embedding impossibility)

Theorem 5.1. There is no function f: \mathbb{R}^5 \to \mathbb{R} that is simultaneously injective, order-preserving, and order-reflecting with respect to the product order \preceq.

Proof. Suppose such f exists. Consider x = (1,0,0,0,0) and y = (0,1,0,0,0). They are incomparable: x \not\preceq y and y \not\preceq x.

Since \mathbb{R} is totally ordered, either f(x) \leq f(y) or f(y) \leq f(x).

Case 1: f(x) \leq f(y). By order-reflection, x \preceq y, contradicting incomparability.

Case 2: f(y) \leq f(x). By order-reflection, y \preceq x, contradicting incomparability.

Both cases yield contradiction. Therefore no such f exists. \square

Remark C.1.1. The theorem does not require injectivity. The argument shows that no order-preserving, order-reflecting map into any totally ordered set exists.

---

C.2. Proof of Proposition 5.1 (Stochastic dominance is a preorder)

Proposition 5.1. The relation \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} on agents is a preorder. It becomes a partial order on the quotient space \mathcal{A}/{\sim} where A \sim B \iff \Theta_A(\lambda) = \Theta_B(\lambda) for all \lambda.

Proof.

Reflexivity. For any A, \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \Theta_A(\lambda) for all \lambda, so A \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} A.

Transitivity. If A \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} B and B \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} C, then for all \lambda: \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \Theta_B(\lambda) \geq \Theta_C(\lambda), so A \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} C.

Thus \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} is a preorder.

Antisymmetry on quotient. Suppose [A] \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} [B] and [B] \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} [A]. Then \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \Theta_B(\lambda) and \Theta_B(\lambda) \geq \Theta_A(\lambda) for all \lambda, so \Theta_A = \Theta_B and A \sim B, hence [A] = [B]. \square

---

C.3. Proposition C.3.1 (Strict stochastic dominance)

Proposition C.3.1. A \succ_{\mathrm{SD}} B if and only if \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \Theta_B(\lambda) for all \lambda and \Theta_A(\lambda_0) > \Theta_B(\lambda_0) for some \lambda_0.

Proof. Immediate from the definition: strict dominance requires weak dominance at every level plus strict dominance at at least one level. \square

---

C.4. Proposition C.4.1 (Pareto frontier of \mathcal{D}_A)

Proposition C.4.1. Under the product order on \mathbb{R}^2,

\operatorname{Pareto}(\mathcal{D}_A) = \{(\lambda, \Theta_A(\lambda)) : \lambda \in \mathbb{R}\} \cap \mathcal{D}_A

when \Theta_A is strictly decreasing.

Proof. For each \lambda, the maximal \theta such that (\lambda, \theta) \in \mathcal{D}_A is \Theta_A(\lambda). The point (\lambda, \Theta_A(\lambda)) is Pareto-optimal if there is no \lambda' > \lambda with \Theta_A(\lambda') > \Theta_A(\lambda), which is exactly the strict-decrease condition. When \Theta_A is constant on an interval, only the right endpoint is Pareto-optimal. \square

