Appendix D — Proofs for Section 7 (Robustness)

D.1. Wasserstein Distance — Standing Assumptions

Assumption D.1. The task cost c: \mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} satisfies:

(i) c is symmetric;

(ii) c is lower semi-continuous with respect to \tau_{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \tau_{\mathcal{T}};

(iii) c(T, T) = 0, and c(T, T') > 0 for T \neq T';

(iv) c is bounded: M_c := \sup_{T, T'} c(T, T') < \infty.

Definition D.1 (Wasserstein distance). For \nu, \mu \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}):

W_c(\nu, \mu) = \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\nu, \mu)} \int_{\mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{T}} c(T, T') \, d\gamma(T, T').

Proposition D.1. Under Assumption D.1, W_c is a metric on \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}).

Proof. This is a standard result in optimal transport (Villani, 2009, Chapter 6). Symmetry, non-negativity, and the triangle inequality follow from the corresponding properties of c and the gluing lemma. Identity of indiscernibles: W_c(\nu, \mu) = 0 iff \nu = \mu, because c(T, T') > 0 for T \neq T' and c is lower semi-continuous, so any optimal coupling must concentrate on the diagonal. \square

---

D.2. Proof of Theorem 7.1 (Monotonicity of robust survival)

Theorem 7.1. For every \lambda and 0 \leq \varepsilon_1 \leq \varepsilon_2:

\Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon_1) \geq \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon_2).

Proof. By Definition 7.1,

\mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_1}(\mu) = \{\nu : W_c(\nu, \mu) \leq \varepsilon_1\} \subseteq \{\nu : W_c(\nu, \mu) \leq \varepsilon_2\} = \mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_2}(\mu).

Since \mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_1} \subseteq \mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_2},

\inf_{\nu \in \mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_2}} \nu(S_\lambda) \leq \inf_{\nu \in \mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_1}} \nu(S_\lambda).

Therefore \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon_2) \leq \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon_1). \square

---

D.3. Proposition D.3.1 (Nominal recovery)

Proposition D.3.1. \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, 0) = \Theta_A(\lambda).

Proof. \mathfrak{U}_0(\mu) = \{\mu\}, so \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, 0) = \mu(S_\lambda) = \Theta_A(\lambda). \square

---

D.4. Proposition D.4.1 (Containment)

Proposition D.4.1. \mathcal{D}_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\varepsilon) \subseteq \mathcal{D}_A(0) for all \varepsilon \geq 0.

Proof. For any \varepsilon \geq 0, \mu \in \mathfrak{U}_\varepsilon(\mu), so

\Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) = \inf_{\nu \in \mathfrak{U}_\varepsilon(\mu)} \nu(S_\lambda) \leq \mu(S_\lambda) = \Theta_A(\lambda).

Thus if \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) \geq \theta, then \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta, so (\lambda, \theta) \in \mathcal{D}_A(0). \square

---

D.5. Proof of Theorem 9.8 (DRO Dual)

Theorem D.5.1 (DRO dual). The primal DRO problem

\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) = \inf_{\nu \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T})} \left\{\mathbb{E}_{T \sim \nu}[\mathbf{1}(M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda)] : W_1(\nu, \widehat{\mu}_n) \leq \varepsilon\right\}

admits the dual representation

\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) = \sup_{\gamma \geq 0} \left\{-\gamma \varepsilon + \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \min_{T' \in \mathcal{T}} \left(\mathbf{1}(M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T') \geq \lambda) + \gamma c(T_i, T')\right)\right\}.

Proof. The primal problem is a linear program over \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}) with a Wasserstein constraint. By Kantorovich duality for W_1 (Villani, 2009, Theorem 5.10), for any bounded continuous function f and measure \nu:

W_1(\nu, \widehat{\mu}_n) = \sup_{\varphi \in \mathrm{Lip}_1} \left\{\int \varphi \, d\nu - \int \varphi \, d\widehat{\mu}_n\right\},

where \mathrm{Lip}_1 is the set of c-Lipschitz functions with respect to c.

The primal problem can be rewritten as

\inf_{\nu} \sup_{\gamma \geq 0} \left\{\int g \, d\nu + \gamma(W_1(\nu, \widehat{\mu}_n) - \varepsilon)\right\},

where g(T) = \mathbf{1}(M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda).

The inner supremum over \gamma \geq 0 enforces the constraint W_1(\nu, \widehat{\mu}_n) \leq \varepsilon as a penalty.

By strong duality (the problem is convex and the constraint set is a compact Wasserstein ball), we interchange inf and sup:

\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) = \sup_{\gamma \geq 0} \left\{-\gamma \varepsilon + \inf_{\nu}\left[\int (g + \gamma \varphi_\nu) \, d\nu - \gamma \int \varphi_\nu \, d\widehat{\mu}_n\right]\right\}.

Applying the Kantorovich dual form of W_1 and interchanging the infimum over \nu with the inner supremum, we obtain

\inf_{\nu}\left[\int g \, d\nu + \gamma W_1(\nu, \widehat{\mu}_n)\right] = \int \min_{T'} (g(T') + \gamma c(T, T')) \, d\widehat{\mu}_n(T).

Substituting g(T') = \mathbf{1}(M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T') \geq \lambda) and replacing the integral over \widehat{\mu}_n = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \delta_{T_i} by a sum yields the stated dual:

\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) = \sup_{\gamma \geq 0} \left\{-\gamma \varepsilon + \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \min_{T'}\left(\mathbf{1}(M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T') \geq \lambda) + \gamma c(T_i, T')\right)\right\}.

\square

Remark D.5.1 (Tractability). The dual is a 1D convex problem in \gamma. The inner minimization over T' is over a finite support set in the empirical case. The objective is concave in \gamma, so standard 1D optimization applies.

