Appendix E — Proofs for Section 9 (Reconstruction)

E.1. Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz Inequality

Theorem E.1 (DKW). Let X_1, \ldots, X_n \sim F be i.i.d. real random variables with CDF F. Let

\widehat{F}_n(t) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbf{1}_{\{X_i \leq t\}}

be the empirical CDF. Then for every \varepsilon > 0:

\Pr\left(\sup_{t \in \mathbb{R}} \left|\widehat{F}_n(t) - F(t)\right| > \varepsilon\right) \leq 2 \exp(-2n\varepsilon^2).

The constant 2 is sharp (Massart, 1990).

---

E.2. Proof of Theorem 9.1 (DKW for \widehat{\Theta}_A)

Theorem 9.1. Under assumptions (MA1)–(MA4), let T_1, \ldots, T_n \sim \mu be i.i.d. task samples. Define

\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}(\lambda) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbf{1}_{\{M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T_i) \geq \lambda\}}.

Then for every \varepsilon > 0:

\Pr\left(\sup_{\lambda \in \mathbb{R}} \left|\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}(\lambda) - \Theta_A(\lambda)\right| > \varepsilon\right) \leq 2 \exp(-2n\varepsilon^2).

Proof. Define X_{A,i} = M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T_i). By Lemma A.2, X_{A,i} is a real-valued random variable. Let F_A(\lambda) = \Pr(X_A \leq \lambda) = 1 - \Theta_A(\lambda), and let \widehat{F}_{A,n}(\lambda) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbf{1}_{\{X_{A,i} \leq \lambda\}}.

Then \widehat{\Theta}_{A,n} = 1 - \widehat{F}_{A,n} and \Theta_A = 1 - F_A, so

\sup_{\lambda} |\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}(\lambda) - \Theta_A(\lambda)| = \sup_{\lambda} |\widehat{F}_{A,n}(\lambda) - F_A(\lambda)|.

Applying Theorem E.1 to the i.i.d. sequence X_{A,1}, \ldots, X_{A,n}:

\Pr\left(\sup_{\lambda} |\widehat{F}_{A,n}(\lambda) - F_A(\lambda)| > \varepsilon\right) \leq 2 \exp(-2n\varepsilon^2).

The result follows. \square

---

E.3. Proof of Corollary 9.1 (Sample complexity)

Corollary 9.1. To achieve uniform estimation error \leq \varepsilon with confidence 1 - \alpha, it suffices to take

n \geq \frac{1}{2\varepsilon^2} \log\left(\frac{2}{\alpha}\right).

Proof. Set 2 \exp(-2n\varepsilon^2) = \alpha. Then

\exp(-2n\varepsilon^2) = \alpha / 2 \implies -2n\varepsilon^2 = \log(\alpha / 2) \implies n = \frac{\log(2/\alpha)}{2\varepsilon^2}.

\square

