E.4. Uniform Glivenko–Cantelli for Survival Class

Proposition E.4.1. The class \mathcal{F} = \{\mathbf{1}_{[\lambda, \infty)} : \lambda \in \mathbb{R}\} is a VC class of dimension 1.

Proof.

Upper bound. A single point x_1 can be shattered: the half-line [\lambda, \infty) contains x_1 if \lambda \leq x_1, and does not if \lambda > x_1.

No shattering of two points. Let x_1 < x_2. Consider the labeling x_1 \mapsto 0, x_2 \mapsto 1. For a half-line [\lambda, \infty) to contain x_2, we need \lambda \leq x_2. For it to exclude x_1, we need \lambda > x_1. So \lambda \in (x_1, x_2]. But then x_1 < \lambda \leq x_2, and x_1 \notin [\lambda, \infty). Contradiction with x_1 \mapsto 1? Actually this labeling is achievable: choose \lambda \in (x_1, x_2], then x_1 \notin [\lambda, \infty) and x_2 \in [\lambda, \infty).

Now consider labeling x_1 \mapsto 1, x_2 \mapsto 0. For x_1 \in [\lambda, \infty), we need \lambda \leq x_1. Then x_2 > x_1 \geq \lambda, so x_2 \in [\lambda, \infty). Contradiction.

Therefore the class cannot shatter two points, so VCdim(\mathcal{F}) = 1. \square

Corollary E.4.2. \mathcal{F} is a uniform Glivenko–Cantelli class, so

\sup_{\lambda} |\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}(\lambda) - \Theta_A(\lambda)| \to 0

almost surely as n \to \infty.

Proof. By the VC theorem (Vapnik–Chervonenkis), a VC class of finite dimension is uniform Glivenko–Cantelli. Combined with the DKW bound, this gives almost-sure convergence. \square

---

E.5. Proof of Proposition E.5.1 (Legg–Hutter non-monotonicity)

Proposition E.5.1. Let \mu_K(T) \propto 2^{-K(T)}. Define \Theta_A^K(\lambda) = \mu_K(\{M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda\}). Then \Theta_A^K is not in general a monotone functional of \Upsilon(A).

Proof. Construct two agents A, B with:

· V^\pi_\mu equal for all \mu with K(\mu) \leq k_0;
· Different V^\pi_\mu for some \mu with K(\mu) > k_0.

Then \Upsilon(A) = \Upsilon(B) because the complexity weighting 2^{-K(\mu)} is dominated by low-complexity environments, and A, B agree there.

However, if the margin field M_{A,H} differs from M_{B,H} on a set of tasks with high complexity but non-negligible \mu_K-mass (e.g., if \mu_K concentrates on a different region than value-weighted \Upsilon), then \Theta_A^K \neq \Theta_B^K.

Concretely: choose tasks T_n with K(T_n) = n and M_A(T_n) = 1, M_B(T_n) = 0. Then

\Theta_A^K(0) = \sum_{n} 2^{-n} = 1, \quad \Theta_B^K(0) = 0.

But \Upsilon(A) = \Upsilon(B) if the value profiles coincide on all environments with complexity \leq n_0 for a fixed n_0. \square

