Appendix F — Details on Task Cost Construction

F.1. Composite Cost Structure

Recall Definition 3.3:

c(T, T') = \alpha \cdot d_{\mathcal{X}}(T, T') + \beta \cdot d_{\mathcal{Y}}(T, T') + \gamma \cdot d_{\mathcal{R}}(T, T').

Proposition F.1. If each of d_{\mathcal{X}}, d_{\mathcal{Y}}, d_{\mathcal{R}} is a pseudo-metric, then c is a pseudo-metric.

Proof. Symmetry: each component is symmetric, so c is symmetric. Triangle inequality: non-negative linear combinations of pseudo-metrics satisfy the triangle inequality. c(T, T) = 0: each component vanishes on the diagonal. \square

Proposition F.2. Under Assumption D.1(iv) and the boundedness of d_{\mathcal{X}}, d_{\mathcal{Y}}, d_{\mathcal{R}}, c is bounded.

Proof. Each component is bounded: d_{\mathcal{X}} \leq M_{\mathcal{X}}, d_{\mathcal{Y}} \leq M_{\mathcal{Y}}, d_{\mathcal{R}} \leq M_{\mathcal{R}}. Then c \leq \alpha M_{\mathcal{X}} + \beta M_{\mathcal{Y}} + \gamma M_{\mathcal{R}}. \square

---

F.2. Domain-Specific Cost Component

Definition F.2.1. The domain-aware cost is

c(T_i, T_j) = w_d \mathbf{1}_{\{\mathrm{domain}(T_i) \neq \mathrm{domain}(T_j)\}} + (1 - w_d) \frac{\|\mathbf{z}(T_i) - \mathbf{z}(T_j)\|_2}{\|\mathbf{z}(T_i)\|_2 + \|\mathbf{z}(T_j)\|_2},

with \mathbf{z}(T) \in \mathbb{R}^3 the intrinsic difficulty vector.

Proposition F.2.2. If w_d \in [0,1] and \mathbf{z}(T) \neq 0, then c is symmetric and positive-definite.

Proof. Symmetry: the indicator and the ratio are symmetric in T_i, T_j. Positive-definiteness: if T_i = T_j, then both terms vanish; if T_i \neq T_j, then either the domains differ (indicator = 1) or the ratio is positive (since \mathbf{z}(T_i) \neq \mathbf{z}(T_j)). \square

---

Appendix G — Details on the Normalization Axiom

G.1. Proof of Proposition G.1 (Human reference at q)

Proposition G.1. Under Axiom N, p_H^{(q)}(T) = q.

Proof. By Axiom N2, \phi_T(s_H^{(q)}) = q. By Definition 3.4 and Axiom N, p_H^{(q)}(T) = \phi_T(s_H^{(q)}). Therefore p_H^{(q)}(T) = q. \square

---

G.2. Examples of Normalization Maps

Example G.2.1 (Rank normalization). Let F_T be the CDF of human scores on T. Set \phi_T(s) = F_T(s). Then \phi_T(s_H^{(q)}) = F_T(F_T^{-1}(q)) = q if F_T is continuous.

Example G.2.2 (Min-max). Let m_T = \min_T s, M_T = \max_T s. Set \phi_T(s) = (s - m_T)/(M_T - m_T).

Example G.2.3 (Quantile transform). \phi_T(s) = F_T(s), with F_T the empirical CDF. Standard in practice.

---

G.3. Proof of Proposition G.3 (Necessity of Axiom N)

Proposition G.3. Without Axiom N, \Theta_A(\lambda) is not invariant under admissible rescaling of raw scores.

Proof. Consider \phi_T and \psi_T = a \phi_T + b with a \neq 1. Then

M_{A,H}^{(q), \psi}(T) = a M_{A,H}^{(q), \phi}(T) + b - (a - 1)q.

The corresponding survival functions are related by \Theta_A^\psi(\lambda) = \Theta_A^\phi((\lambda - b + (a-1)q)/a). This rescales the horizontal axis, changing the shape of \mathcal{D}_A. Axiom N prevents arbitrary rescaling by pinning p_H^{(q)}(T) = q. \square

---

Appendix H — Details on the Autonomy Profile

H.1. Proof of Proposition H.1 (Non-scalarization)

Proposition H.1. Any scalarization A_A = \sum_k w_k A_k with w_k > 0 introduces an ordering principle not determined by the autonomy profile \mathbf{A}_A.

Proof. Consider \mathbf{A} = (1, 0, 0, 0) and \mathbf{B} = (0, 1, 0, 0). Under the product order on \mathbb{R}^4, they are incomparable: \mathbf{A} \not\preceq \mathbf{B} and \mathbf{B} \not\preceq \mathbf{A}. A scalarization with w_0 > w_1 ranks \mathbf{A} \succ \mathbf{B}. A scalarization with w_1 > w_0 ranks \mathbf{B} \succ \mathbf{A}. The scalar ranking depends on the arbitrary weights. \square

---

H.2. Properties of Autonomy-Constrained Dominance

Definition H.2.1.

\Theta_A^{\mathrm{auto}}(\lambda, k) = \mu\left(\left\{T : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda \text{ and } \mathcal{A}(T) \geq k\right\}\right).

Proposition H.2.1. \Theta_A^{\mathrm{auto}}(\lambda, k) is non-increasing in k.

Proof. If k_1 \leq k_2, then \{T : \mathcal{A}(T) \geq k_2\} \subseteq \{T : \mathcal{A}(T) \geq k_1\}, so the intersection with S_\lambda is smaller and the measure is no larger. \square

Proposition H.2.2. \Theta_A^{\mathrm{auto}}(\lambda, 0) = \Theta_A(\lambda).

Proof. Since \mathcal{A}(T) \geq 0 for all T by definition of the autonomy axis, the constraint \mathcal{A}(T) \geq 0 is vacuous. Therefore \Theta_A^{\mathrm{auto}}(\lambda, 0) = \mu(S_\lambda) = \Theta_A(\lambda). \square

---

H.3. Multi-Axis Autonomy

Definition H.3.1. Let \mathcal{A}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{A}_m be m distinct autonomy axes. The multi-axis autonomy profile is

\mathbf{A}_A = (A_{j,k})_{j \in [m], k \in \{0,1,2,3\}}, \quad A_{j,k} = \mu(\{T : \mathcal{A}_j(T) \geq k\}).

Remark H.3.1. The profile \mathbf{A}_A is a matrix of size m \times 4. Any scalarization requires both axis weights and level weights, doubling the arbitrariness. We therefore retain the profile.

