Appendix I — Additional Technical Results

I.1. Dominance Quantile Function

Definition I.1.1. Q_A(\theta) = \sup\{\lambda : \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta\}.

Proposition I.1.1. Q_A is non-increasing and right-continuous.

Proof. Standard properties of generalized inverses of monotone functions. \square

---

I.2. Empirical Estimation of Q_A

Definition I.2.1. \widehat{Q}_{A,n}(\theta) = \inf\{\lambda : \widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}(\lambda) < \theta\}.

Proposition I.2.1. \widehat{Q}_{A,n}(\theta) is a consistent estimator of Q_A(\theta) at points of continuity of Q_A.

Proof. By the DKW inequality, \widehat{\Theta}_{A,n} \to \Theta_A uniformly with probability 1. The quantile function is continuous at points where \Theta_A is strictly decreasing. Therefore \widehat{Q}_{A,n} \to Q_A at points of continuity. \square

---

I.3. Numerical Algorithm for \widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}

Algorithm I.3.1 (Empirical dominance survival function).

Input. Samples X_{A,1}, \ldots, X_{A,n}; grid \lambda_1 < \cdots < \lambda_m.

Output. \widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}(\lambda_j) for j = 1, \ldots, m.

Procedure.

1. Sort samples: X_{(1)} \leq \cdots \leq X_{(n)}.
2. For each j, compute \widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}(\lambda_j) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_i \mathbf{1}_{\{X_i \geq \lambda_j\}}.
3. Return (\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}(\lambda_j))_{j=1}^m.

Complexity. O(n \log n + nm).

Uniform error. By Theorem 9.1, with probability 1 - \alpha:

\sup_j |\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}(\lambda_j) - \Theta_A(\lambda_j)| \leq \sqrt{\frac{1}{2n} \log\left(\frac{2}{\alpha}\right)}.

