Appendix J — Numerical Verification Example

To verify the implementation of the empirical estimator and the DRO dual, we consider a minimal numerical example.

J.1. Setup

Four tasks with uniform measure \mu(T_i) = 1/4. Margins:

Task M_A(T_i) M_B(T_i)
T_1 0.30 0.10
T_2 0.20 0.15
T_3 0.10 0.40
T_4 -0.10 0.35

J.2. Compute \Theta_A, \Theta_B

Sorted margins for A: -0.10, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30.

\Theta_A(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq -0.10, \\ 3/4 & -0.10 < \lambda \leq 0.10, \\ 2/4 & 0.10 < \lambda \leq 0.20, \\ 1/4 & 0.20 < \lambda \leq 0.30, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.30. \end{cases}

Sorted margins for B: 0.10, 0.15, 0.35, 0.40.

\Theta_B(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq 0.10, \\ 3/4 & 0.10 < \lambda \leq 0.15, \\ 2/4 & 0.15 < \lambda \leq 0.35, \\ 1/4 & 0.35 < \lambda \leq 0.40, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.40. \end{cases}

J.3. Dominance Comparison

Pointwise comparison:

· At \lambda = 0.05: \Theta_A = 3/4, \Theta_B = 1. B dominates.
· At \lambda = 0.25: \Theta_A = 1/4, \Theta_B = 2/4. B dominates.
· At \lambda = -0.15: both = 1.

Thus \Theta_B(\lambda) \geq \Theta_A(\lambda) for all \lambda, so B \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} A.

J.4. Robustness Illustration

Suppose \mathfrak{U}_\varepsilon(\mu) is a Wasserstein ball of radius \varepsilon = 0.1. Worst-case measure for A: concentrates on T_4 where M_A = -0.10. Then

\Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(0, \varepsilon) = \nu(M_A \geq 0) = 0.

Worst-case for B: concentrates on T_1 where M_B = 0.10. Then

\Theta_B^{\mathrm{rob}}(0, \varepsilon) = 1.

Thus B is robustly dominant at \lambda = 0, while A is not.

