Appendix L — Assumptions and Their Necessity

We verify that each standing assumption is necessary for at least one theorem.

Axiom N (Normalization compatibility). Without N, the dominance survival function \Theta_A is not invariant under rescaling of raw scores (Proposition G.3), so \mathcal{D}_A is not well-defined as an object independent of task-specific scale.

Axiom T1 (Non-degeneracy). Without T1, the task space may concentrate on a measure-zero subset, invalidating the notion of coverage.

Axiom T2 (Representation invariance). Without T2, dominance classification becomes representation-dependent, contradicting the architecture-independence requirement.

Axiom T3 (Difficulty sensitivity). Without T3, \Theta_A cannot distinguish tasks by difficulty, so the trade-off between intensity and breadth is not meaningful.

Axiom T4 (Domain coverage). Without T4, a specialized agent could achieve high coverage by ignoring entire cognitive domains.

Axiom T5 (Stability). Without T5, the classification of an agent as superintelligent could reverse under infinitesimal task-generation changes.

Assumption MA3 (Joint measurability). Necessary for Lemma A.2 (measurability of the margin field).

Assumption D.1 (Cost regularity). Necessary for W_c to be a metric (Proposition D.1) and for the DRO dual (Theorem D.5.1).

Assumption 4.0a (Joint measurability of \phi_T). Necessary for Lemma A.2.

