Appendix M — Computational Notes on the DRO Dual

M.1. Solving the Dual

The dual problem is

\widehat{\Theta}_{A,n}^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) = \sup_{\gamma \geq 0} \left\{-\gamma \varepsilon + \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n g_i(\gamma)\right\},

where g_i(\gamma) = \min_{j \in [n]} \left(\mathbf{1}_{M_j \geq \lambda} + \gamma c(T_i, T_j)\right).

Convexity. Each g_i is a minimum of affine functions in \gamma, hence concave. The sum is concave. The overall objective is concave in \gamma. The supremum over \gamma \geq 0 is a 1D concave maximization.

Algorithm. Standard 1D optimization (golden-section search or Brent's method) over \gamma \in [0, \gamma_{\max}], where \gamma_{\max} is chosen such that \gamma_{\max} \varepsilon > 1.

Complexity per \lambda. O(n^2) for the inner minimization, O(K) for the outer 1D search with K iterations. Total O(K n^2).

M.2. Interpretation of \gamma

The dual multiplier \gamma represents the marginal cost of enlarging the ambiguity set. Small \gamma corresponds to trusting the empirical measure; large \gamma corresponds to large perturbation of the measure.

M.3. Monotonicity in \varepsilon

The supremum representation makes monotonicity in \varepsilon evident: increasing \varepsilon enlarges the penalty -\gamma \varepsilon, which shifts the optimizer toward smaller \gamma and reduces the value of the supremum.

